Assume the countable sets are indexed by $NN$: given countable sets $A_1, A_2, A_3, ...$, list each as
$ A_i = {a_1^((i)), a_2^((i)), a_3^((i)), ...}. $
Define $f: union.big_(n in NN) A_n -> NN$ by
$
  a_j^((i)) |-> 2^(i) times 3^(j),
$
taking the least index $i$ when an element lies in several $A_i$ so that $f$ is well-defined. As with $NN times NN$, uniqueness of prime factorisation shows $f$ is an injection, so $union.big_(n in NN) A_n$ is countable.
